<%!-- A user-mode program, explained: what the machine has (no kernel), how the program is doing, and
    commands to try, each saying what armless answers (nokernel.js) --%>
    <div class="pane pane-side">
      <div class="pane-bar"><span class="pane-name">user mode</span><span class="pane-note">vm.user_status</span></div>
      <div class="side-body">
        <dl class="figures">
          <div><dt>guest kernel</dt><dd>none</dd></div>
          <div><dt>sh first wrote after</dt><dd><span data-started>-</span><small>ms</small></dd></div>
          <div><dt>sh is</dt><dd class="word" data-sh>-</dd></div>
          <div><dt>files fetched</dt><dd data-files>0</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <h3 class="side-h">try, and what answers</h3>
        <ul class="tries">
          <li><button type="button" class="btn" data-send="uname -a&#10;" disabled>uname -a</button><p class="note">armless answers as Linux "6.6.0-armless-user": no Linux is running.</p></li>
          <li><button type="button" class="btn" data-send="ps&#10;" disabled>ps</button><p class="note">reads /proc, which armless keeps: sh, and a fork of it running ps (busybox runs its applets in a fork, without exec).</p></li>
          <li><button type="button" class="btn" data-send="cat /proc/self/status&#10;" disabled>cat /proc/self/status</button><p class="note">the memory armless counts for that cat, page by page as it touches it.</p></li>
          <li><button type="button" class="btn" data-send="ls -l /bin | head&#10;" disabled>ls -l /bin</button><p class="note">the filesystem: busybox and its links, each file fetched the first time it is read.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
